# BASE destination only — the chunk backend on Unraid (`kamal deploy`, no -d).
#
# ⚠️ THIS FILE IS IGNORED UNDER `-d`. Kamal reads [.kamal/secrets-common, .kamal/secrets.<dest>],
# so adding a secret here does nothing for s3/share/ovh — it must go in that destination's own
# file. Shared keys (RAILS_MASTER_KEY, KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD) live in .kamal/secrets-common.
#
# Safe for git: references and commands only, never a raw credential.

# CHUNK_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY WAS HERE: the SeaweedFS credential (identity `seaweednew`) for the
# embedded chunk store, which left with the sync engine (Arc D Part 2). config/deploy.yml declares
# it no longer, and InlineCommandSubstitution runs every $(...) in a parsed file EAGERLY -- so
# leaving it would make every deploy shell out to 1Password for a value no container receives.
# The `-d ovh` copy, which resolved the SAME NAME to a Cloudflare R2 token, is gone for the same
# reason. ⚠️ The older `seaweedfs` 1Password item is RETIRED (it belonged to the dead .7 box) --
# signing with it fails InvalidAccessKeyId. `seaweednew` is the live one.
#
# THE RULE THAT PAIR EARNED OUTLIVES IT: one variable name meant two different credentials on two
# destinations, which is why references live per-destination and NOT in secrets-common, where
# nothing could tell them apart (docs/invariants/deploy-secrets.md, §14g).

# SYNC_ADMIN_TOKEN — REQUIRED HERE TWICE OVER, AND THIS IS THE ONE FILE WHERE BOTH REASONS APPLY.
# (1) The `sync` accessory (config/deploy.yml, Arc D Task 2) declares it as ITS OWN secret and
# `accessories:` is never overridden by any destination overlay, so every destination's MERGED
# config names it (verified against Kamal::Configuration.raw_config for all four, not assumed;
# test/deploy_hygiene_test.rb re-derives it every run). (2) Unlike share/s3/ovh, THIS destination's
# maker app also SENDS it: the base carries LIBRARY_BACKEND=chunk_remote since Arc D Part 2, and
# FileLayer::ChunkRemote::Provisioning calls POST /v1/admin/libraries with it. This comment used to
# say the opposite — "this destination runs plain FileLayer::Chunk" — which was true only while the
# base was the embedded-chunk one and a separate `remote` overlay carried chunk_remote.
#
# Deliberately NOT in .kamal/secrets-common (§14g): that file's `$(…)` lines run eagerly for every
# destination that reads it, and reason (2) is a genuine per-destination story that SECRET_KEY_BASE
# (which IS in secrets-common — see that file's own comment) does not have. Repeating the reference
# per-destination keeps that distinction visible rather than blurring it into "just another common
# key". The fullest statement of the argument lives with the accessory, in config/deploy.yml.
SYNC_ADMIN_TOKEN=$(bin/secret SYNC_ADMIN_TOKEN 'op://tinkerex/tinkerex-sync-admin-token/password')
